{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed218530468/10/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"902510d5\" lines=\"58-80\">\n<description>\nKeep the largest 8-connected motif and replace the corner/control pixels with a solid corner triangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Exactly one grid corner contains a foreground control pixel; it gives the\n  corner from which the triangle is painted.\n- There is one unique largest 8-connected monochrome foreground object, the\n  motif that remains in the output; all other foreground pixels are controls.\n- Every foreground component outside that motif is a singleton control pixel.\n- The fill color is the unique control color that appears in at least two\n  non-corner control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Start from a blank background canvas and redraw only the largest motif.\n- Count the non-corner control pixels; that count is the triangle side.\n- Paint the fill color on the right-triangle region where those two distances\n  from the marked corner's edges sum to less than the side length.\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered singleton controls are counted into a compact triangular\n  stack at the marked corner; only the largest motif is not part of the count.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"11dc524f\" lines=\"25-40\">\n<description>\nSlide the non-square sprite to the square anchor and replace the anchor with the sprite's mirror image.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- There is one 8-connected non-background sprite that is not a solid 2x2 square.\n- There is one solid 2x2 square anchor in another non-background color.\n\nRule:\n- Move the sprite straight toward the anchor along their unique cardinal separation until it touches the anchor's near edge.\n- Reflect the moved sprite across that near edge and draw the reflected copy in the anchor color.\n- Clear the original locations; the output keeps only the moved sprite and reflected anchor-color copy.\n\nKey insight:\n- The 2x2 square marks the mirror line: its side facing the sprite becomes the contact edge for a two-color reflected pair.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c920a713\" lines=\"87-102\">\n<description>\nUntangle overlapping colored rectangular frames into concentric square frames ordered by their visible crossings.\n\nInput structure\n- The background contains several one-cell-thick axis-aligned rectangular frames, one color per frame.\n- Frames overlap, and the visible color at an overlap records a directed crossing from the hidden frame to the visible frame.\n- Each foreground color still has visible pixels on the perimeter of its full rectangular bounding box.\n\nRule\n- Infer each color's full rectangular frame from the bounding box of its visible pixels.\n- Add a directed edge A -> B when B is visible at a crossing of A and B's frame perimeters.\n- Find the unique path that visits every frame color once, then render those colors as one-pixel-thick concentric square frames from outside to inside.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a crop of the input; the crossing graph gives a single frame order, and that order is redrawn as nested rings.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6a980be1\" lines=\"46-61\">\n<description>\nProject the framed periodic stripe pattern across the canvas and erase the unused frame cells.\n\nInput structure\n- A bg canvas with a one-cell-thick rectangular frame of one color.\n- Inside the frame are two parallel full-interior marker bars of another color.\n- A third color fills the region between the marker bars on periodically spaced rows or columns.\n\nRule\n- Infer the periodic row or column mask from the third-color stripes inside the frame.\n- Paint the frame color along those full rows or columns, extended to the grid boundary.\n- Extend the marker bars across the full canvas and repaint the source-color stripe cells between them.\n\nKey insight\n- The outer frame is not preserved as a border; it supplies the color for the periodic stripes selected by the inner source pattern.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e12f9a14\" lines=\"138-152\">\n<description>\nEmit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks through frame gaps; beams interact on collision.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall color (e.g. green) forms 4x4 frames around 2x2 monochrome blocks.\n- Each frame has one or more gap pixels where the wall is missing.\n\nRule:\n- Each gap emits a beam of the inner block's color, traveling diagonally outward (direction set by gap position relative to the block).\n- Beams paint every cell they cross until they hit the grid edge or collide.\n- 90° collision: both beams turn 45° toward their bisector direction; the intersection cell is left blank (1-pixel gap).\n- 45° collision: beams merge into a zigzag (alternating cardinal + diagonal steps) with no gap at the meeting point.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct collision rules — 90° produces a gap and single-pixel diagonal lines; 45° produces no gap and thicker zigzag bands.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7c66cb00\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDock two-tone sprites into color-matched horizontal bars with a color swap.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Top area: small two-tone sprites (outer color + inner color).\n- Below: horizontal bars spanning most of the row, each with a fill color and border color on edges.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the bar whose fill color equals the sprite's outer color.\n- Place the sprite at its original x-position, aligned to the bar's bottom.\n- Recolor: sprite outer → bar border, sprite inner → bar fill.\n- Erase original sprites from the top area.\n\nKey insight: Color matching is inverted — the sprite's dominant color identifies its target bar, then gets swapped to the bar's accent color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5961cc34\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nBuild a red ray network by activating blue blobs via directional green ports.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Large blue blobs with adjacent green pixels marking port directions.\n- Start marker: yellow pixel atop a short red segment at grid bottom.\n\nRule:\n- Extend a ray upward from start; on hitting a blue blob, recolor it red.\n- Activated blobs emit thick rays through green ports until boundary or another blob.\n- Cascade until no new blobs are reached.\n\nKey insight: Green pixels encode ray direction and width; the network activates as a chain reaction from one start point.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a2d730bd\" lines=\"43-56\">\n<description>\nExtend each solid foreground rectangle toward its same-color isolated pixels with capped one-cell paths, clearing each original marker pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each foreground color has exactly one solid rectangular object and one or more isolated same-color marker pixels.\n- Every marker is horizontally or vertically aligned with the rectangle bbox and lies outside it.\nRule\n- For each marker, draw a one-cell-wide straight path from the adjacent side of the rectangle through one cell beyond the marker.\n- Add a three-pixel perpendicular cap at the rectangle end and around the marker endpoint.\n- Recolor the original marker pixel to background.\nKey insight\n- The isolated pixels are not copied; they mark the hollow endpoints of capped protrusions grown from their matching rectangle.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f560132c\" lines=\"259-273\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the \"pieces\"), scattered across the grid.\n- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.\n\nRule:\n- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.\n- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.\n- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.\n\nKey insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"64efde09\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nCopy colored control-ray ports from one rectangular sprite orientation to every matching transformed sprite.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg is the most common color.\n- Several dense 2-by-N or N-by-2 rectangular sprites are dihedral transforms of one template.\n- Isolated single-pixel control markers sit on rays projected outward from the dominant long edge of some sprites.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, the active edge is the long side whose cells have the strongest same-color majority.\n- A control marker seen first along an outward ray from an active-edge cell assigns its color to that template cell.\n- On every transformed sprite, draw that assigned color outward from the corresponding active-edge cell through bg cells until blocked or out of bounds.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marker positions are not absolute; they label cells of a shared rectangular template, then the labels follow each sprite's rotation/reflection.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"83b6b474\" lines=\"95-111\">\n<description>\nTranslate the colored objects into the most balanced complete rectangular frame.\n\nInput structure\nA grid with one dominant background color and several monochromatic\n4-connected foreground objects that are straight bars or corner-like line\nsprites.\n\nRule\nPreserve every foreground object exactly, allowing translation only, and\nplace the objects so they cover every pixel of a one-cell-thick rectangular\nframe exactly once; the frame interior is the background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe output shape is not copied from the input: the foreground pixel count\ndetermines possible frame sizes, and the intended output is the valid frame\nassembly with the least difference between height and width.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
